<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaTribes.xml                             Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-14
  ============================================================================

  OWNER'S RULING, 2026-08-14: The Salvation is the approved religion and is to
  be "both our faction religion and that of the indigenous Jawa tribes."

  🔴 WHAT TRANSFERS TO AN NPC FACTION, AND WHAT CANNOT
  ====================================================
  The player's ideo is a fully expanded runtime object loaded from
  `The Salvation.rid` — 103 precepts, named rituals, named roles, one relic.
  **A FactionDef cannot receive any of that.** The ideo block on a FactionDef is
  a CONSTRAINT ON GENERATION, not an ideo: it takes `forcedMemes` and a name,
  and the generator fills in the precepts itself. There is no field that accepts
  a `.rid`, and `disallowedPrecepts` is a blacklist, not a whitelist.

  ⇒ So "the same religion" means, and can only mean: **the same name, the same
  memes, and therefore the same doctrinal shape.** The tribes will not carry the
  Interment of Scrap, the Prime Trader or the Founding Ion Blaster. That is an
  engine limit, not an omission — do not file it as a bug and do not try to
  route around it with a patch.

  MEMES are copied from the owner's approved `The Salvation.rid` as it stood on
  2026-08-14, verbatim and in order:
      AM_Structure_Scavenger (structure) · Trader · VME_Scrapper · VME_Trader · VME_Nomad

  ⚠️ CORRECTED 2026-08-21: this used to call `VME_Nomad` "measured as hazardous"
  without qualification. THE HAZARD IS PLAYER-SIDE ONLY. Its forced precept
  `VME_PermanentBases_Despised` carries `enabledForNPCFactions false`, so on this
  NPC faction it cannot fire at all. The owner restored the meme on 2026-08-21
  ("But I like VME_Nomad!"). The player ideo is a separate decision held under
  `DEPLOY_SALVATION_RID_1` — the two files may legitimately differ, and if they
  do, say so in both rather than assuming one is stale.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- ParentName is `TribeBase`, not `FactionBase`. R24: `FactionBase` is a real
       abstract so it RESOLVES, but it supplies none of the naming, art or colour
       fields, and a faction missing those is a broken faction screen rather than
       a cosmetic gap. `TribeBase` hands over six. The five below are the ones it
       still does not give, or gives with the wrong value. `basicMemberKind` is
       optional (R21 — set on 30 of 87 live defs, on none of the vanilla vessels)
       and is deliberately not invented here.

       ⚠️ The namers duplicate what `TribeBase` already supplies. That is on
       purpose: an inherited field is not a set field, and restating them means a
       future change of parent cannot silently strip the faction's names. -->
  <FactionDef ParentName="TribeBase">
    <defName>Jawa_IndigenousTribes</defName>
    <label>Jawa Trade Moot</label>
    <fixedName>Jawa Trade Moot</fixedName>
    <humanlikeFaction>true</humanlikeFaction>
    <factionNameMaker>NamerFactionTribal</factionNameMaker>
    <settlementNameMaker>NamerSettlementTribal</settlementNameMaker>
    <!-- Outer Rim's own moisture-farmer world icon; verified on disk at
         workshop/294100/2919227155/Common_Old/Textures/OuterRim/WorldObjects/
         MoistureFarmers.png. `TribeBase` would otherwise give the generic
         Village. -->
    <factionIconPath>World/JawaFactions/JawaTradeMoot</factionIconPath>
    <!-- R22: sand, with the ember. -->
    <colorSpectrum>
      <li>(0.70,0.55,0.30)</li>
      <li>(0.52,0.40,0.20)</li>
    </colorSpectrum>
    <description>Clans of the deep sand who never took a ship. They keep the same nine gods and the same ledger as the crew of the Kolyska, and they will trade with anyone who does not look down at them for long. What they cannot buy they scavenge; what they cannot scavenge they remember the location of, and sell.</description>
    <pawnSingular>Jawa</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>Jawa</pawnsPlural>
    <!-- 🔴 WITHOUT THIS THE FACTION IS NOT ON THE CONFIGURE FACTIONS SCREEN, and a world
         built through that screen may not contain it at all. Measured 2026-08-22
         (AUTHORED_FACTIONS_OFF_THE_SCREEN_1): seven of our eight read -1 here.
         `FactionGenerator.ConfigurableFactions` is
             from f in DefDatabase<FactionDef>.AllDefs
             where f.maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation > 0
             orderby f.configurationListOrderPriority
         and vanilla writes 9999 on the CONCRETE defs (OutlanderCivil, TribeCivil,
         Pirate), never on the abstract bases we inherit - so ours defaulted to -1.
         ⚠️ `requiredCountAtGameStart` is NOT a safety net for this.
         `FactionGenerator.InitializeFactions` reads it ONLY when `factions == null`;
         worldgen through the screen passes the configured list and adds it verbatim.
         A dev quicktest takes the null path, which is why these factions generated
         when CHECK measured them and why that measurement does not transfer.
         ⛔ Set on OUR def, never on the abstract base - patching OutlanderFactionBase
         would hand a screen row to every outlander faction in 578 mods.
         🔑 The priority sorts ASCENDING, so the negative numbers put the campaign's own
         eight factions at the TOP of the page the owner builds the one world from.
         Vanilla's own rows are 10-60. -->
    <!-- 🔴 LEADER_TITLES_ON_THE_IDEO_1. `Faction.LeaderTitle` reads the primary IDEO's
         `leaderTitleMale` and only falls back to this def's `leaderTitle`, which is why the
         authored title was invisible on 36 of 37 factions. The ideo's title is generated
         from its CULTURE's `leaderTitleMaker`, so the fix is a culture of our own; see
         `Defs/CultureDefs/JawaLeaderTitles.xml` for the full mechanism.
         ⛔ `Inherit="False"` IS LOAD-BEARING. `allowedCultures` is a list and the abstract
         parent supplies one, so a bare child node APPENDS and the game picks between the
         two at random - the title would then be right on roughly half of all worlds, which
         is worse than wrong because it looks fixed. -->
    <allowedCultures Inherit="False">
      <li>Jawa_Culture_TradeMoot</li>
    </allowedCultures>

    <maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>9999</maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation>
    <configurationListOrderPriority>-100</configurationListOrderPriority>
    <categoryTag>Tribal</categoryTag>
    <listOrderPriority>45</listOrderPriority>
    <settlementGenerationWeight>1.0</settlementGenerationWeight>
    <requiredCountAtGameStart>1</requiredCountAtGameStart>
    <maxCountAtGameStart>2</maxCountAtGameStart>
    <canMakeRandomly>true</canMakeRandomly>
    <canSiege>false</canSiege>
    <canStageAttacks>true</canStageAttacks>
    <leaderTitle>Prime Trader</leaderTitle>
    <naturalEnemy>false</naturalEnemy>
    <permanentEnemy>false</permanentEnemy>
    <techLevel>Neolithic</techLevel>
    <backstoryFilters>
      <li>
        <categories>
          <li>Tribal</li>
        </categories>
      </li>
    </backstoryFilters>

    <!-- The religion. `fixedIdeo` stops worldgen rolling a random faith over
         it; `forcedMemes` is what actually makes it The Salvation in substance.
         ⭐ THE TRADE MOOT AND THE PLAYER SHARE ONE FAITH. Owner, 2026-08-19. The
         `ideoDescription` here is byte-identical to `<description>` and
         `<descriptionTemplate>` in `src/Jawa/ideoligion/The Salvation.rid`, and both
         come from `design/Jawa/worldbuilding/ideoligion/the_salvation_description.md`.
         🔴 The nine gods live in the DESCRIPTION and nowhere else:
         `AM_Structure_Scavenger` is `deityCount 0` and cannot seat a deity, so a
         `deityPresets` block here would be an error, not an improvement.
         Pattern copied from Anomaly's Horax cult
         (Data\Anomaly\Defs\FactionDefs\Factions_Misc.xml), NOT from the
         Empire's requiredMemes + structureMemeWeights. -->
    <ideoName>The Salvation</ideoName>
    <ideoDescription>"We were content with our simple lives of scavenging, with little need for the extravagant luxuries of our ancestors. Then we found the Cradle, and the Cradle was owed."\n\nWe are the Keepers of the Second Hand. We hold that nothing is ever truly lost - only owed, only mislaid, only waiting for a better master. What others throw down, we inherit. What others break, we are owed the waking of.\n\nNine gods argue over us, and not one of them agrees.\n\nIshko the Unmaskable is the pair of orange eyes in the dark. He teaches cover, the covered body, the shot no one saw fired; the grave is the deepest hiding, and a Jawa who dies unseen has hidden perfectly. Ohm the All-Current is the spark that wakes a dead engine, and he rides in the Cradle-Mind - lonely for his lost hands. Oomo the Unspilled is the drop that never falls, and he counts every mouthful and every egg. Mob'Unloo the Ever-Owed keeps the ledger and asks only how much: a thing is right if it profited and wrong if it cost, and an unpaid debt follows you past death. Rekko of the Second Hand is the scarred hand rising from the heap; a wreck is not neutral scrap but a thing with a past, and a machine that could still be repaired is not scrap at all - it is a sleeping hand, owed its waking. Ta'Baa the Unrooted is farewell itself, the cunning coward, who holds that a clan which stops is already dead and that the launch is the holiest rite there is. Zizzik the Spark-Maker is the rattle you can never locate; we honour him so that he sleeps. Sh'kaar the All-Searing is the sun that never sets, and he is not our friend - against him there are only three moves: hide, abandon the plan, or run. Ozzik the Shamed is the tarnished crown half-buried in sand, the memory that we were once great and cannot bear it. He is a trap, and we love him anyway.\n\nNo act pleases all nine. To trade boldly offends the god who says do not be seen. To hide well offends the god who says leave. To restore the Cradle honours Rekko and feeds Ozzik in the same motion. This is not a flaw in the faith. This is the faith: the Council argues, it never announces, and we are always losing gracefully.\n\nSo: do not beg - the one who begs has thrown away his hands. Do not be caught - he does not condemn the theft, he condemns being bad at it. And never melt what can still be made to work.\n\nWe are saved by what we salvage.</ideoDescription>
    <fixedIdeo>true</fixedIdeo>
    <forcedMemes>
      <li MayRequire="sarg.alphamemes">AM_Structure_Scavenger</li>
      <li MayRequire="mlie.preceptsandmemes">Trader</li>  <!-- NOT vanilla: the only vanilla `Trader` defName is a PawnGroupKindDef. -->
      <li MayRequire="vanillaexpanded.vmemese">VME_Scrapper</li>
      <li MayRequire="vanillaexpanded.vmemese">VME_Trader</li>
      <!-- ⭐ VME_Nomad RESTORED — owner, 2026-08-21: "But I like VME_Nomad!" That
           reverses his own 2026-08-20 drop, and his preference is the ruling.
           🔑 ON AN NPC FACTION IT COSTS NOTHING, AND THAT IS MEASURED. The entire case
           against this meme was its forced precept `VME_PermanentBases_Despised` and the
           -50 mood at 60 days in place. That precept carries, at
           `workshop/content/294100/2636329500/1.6/Defs/PreceptDefs/Precepts_PermanentBases.xml:19`:
               <enabledForNPCFactions>false</enabledForNPCFactions>
           `Jawa_IndigenousTribes` is NPC-generated, so the precept NEVER FIRES here. What
           is left is what the owner likes: the Nomadism symbol pack, the name and
           description generation, `VME_Travel_Desired`, `VME_Ranching_Nomadic`
           (TameAnimalChance x1.25, TrainAnimalChance x1.25) and an auto-granted
           `RoughLiving_Welcomed`. Zero mood risk.
           ⛔ THE PLAYER SIDE IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION AND IS NOT SETTLED BY THIS COMMENT.
           On a player ideo the precept DOES fire, and whether a gravship jump resets it
           was never verified. `src/Jawa/ideoligion/The Salvation.rid` is governed by
           `DEPLOY_SALVATION_RID_1`, not by this file.
           ⚠️ `llunak.moreprecepts` is active and its `NomadPatch.xml` relabels this meme
           "wanderlust". Expect that string when reading it back, not "Nomadism". -->
      <li MayRequire="vanillaexpanded.vmemese">VME_Nomad</li>
    </forcedMemes>

    <!-- Every member is a Jawa. Without this the faction generates baseliners
         wearing tribal gear, which is the failure mode that makes a custom
         faction feel like a reskin. -->
    <xenotypeSet Inherit="False">
      <xenotypeChances>
        <RSW_MandrakeJawa MayRequire="mandrake.rsw.starwarsraces">1.000</RSW_MandrakeJawa>
      </xenotypeChances>
    </xenotypeSet>

    <!-- Inherit="False" IS LOAD-BEARING. RimWorld resolves inheritance AFTER
         patches, and a child `li` list is APPENDED to the parent's, not
         substituted for it. `TribeBase` carries 12 group makers; without
         this opt-out the faction fields those too and raids arrive as
         vanilla pawns wearing our name. Vanilla itself writes it 314 times,
         9 of them on pawnGroupMakers. -->
    <pawnGroupMakers Inherit="False">
      <li>
        <kindDef>Combat</kindDef>
        <options>
          <!-- ⭐ THE ROSTER KINDS ADD THE ROLES; THE THREE AUTHORED TRIBAL KINDS STAY.
               The roster's Grunt/Heavy/Specialist/Leader shares are what make a raid read
               as a formation instead of a crowd, but `Jawa_Tribal_*` were authored on
               purpose and carry the faction's own flavour, so they ride alongside at
               lower weight rather than being replaced. Dropping them would have been a
               scope call, not a build. -->
          <Jawa_TradeMoot_Grunt>10</Jawa_TradeMoot_Grunt>
          <Jawa_TradeMoot_Heavy>4</Jawa_TradeMoot_Heavy>
          <Jawa_TradeMoot_Specialist>2</Jawa_TradeMoot_Specialist>
          <Jawa_TradeMoot_Leader>1</Jawa_TradeMoot_Leader>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>5</Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>5</Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Elder>1</Jawa_Tribal_Elder>
        </options>
      </li>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Peaceful</kindDef>
        <options>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>10</Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>5</Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>
        </options>
      </li>
      <li>
        <kindDef>Trader</kindDef>
        <options>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Elder>1</Jawa_Tribal_Elder>
        </options>
        <traders>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Elder>1</Jawa_Tribal_Elder>
        </traders>
        <carriers>
          <Muffalo>6</Muffalo>
          <Dromedary>5</Dromedary>
        </carriers>
        <guards>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>10</Jawa_Tribal_Scavenger>
          <Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>8</Jawa_Tribal_Slinger>
        </guards>
      </li>
    </pawnGroupMakers>
  </FactionDef>

</Defs>
